    <section>
        <div class="card">
            <h2>🧠 1.1 Definition of Psychology & Related Concepts</h2>
            
            <p>The word <strong>psychology</strong> comes from two Greek words: <span class="highlight-purple">psyche (Ψ)</span> meaning <em>mind, soul, or spirit</em>, and <span class="highlight-purple">logos</span> meaning <em>study, knowledge, or discourse</em>.</p>
            
            <p><strong>Psychology</strong> is formally defined as the <span class="highlight-green">scientific study</span> of human <span class="highlight-orange">behavior</span> and underlying <span class="highlight-blue">mental processes</span>.</p>

            <h3>🔬 Science</h3>
            <p>Psychology is a <strong>science</strong> because it relies on:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><span class="highlight-green">Scientific procedures</span> and structured experiments</li>
                <li><span class="highlight-green">Empirical data</span> (observable and measurable evidence)</li>
                <li>Avoiding reliance on guesswork, intuition, or common sense</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="example-box">
                💡 <strong>Example:</strong> Instead of assuming "girls are more emotional than boys" based on personal opinion, a psychologist collects objective data using standardized emotional measurement tools.
            </div>

            <h3>🚶 Behavior</h3>
            <p>Behavior includes all actions and reactions that can be categorized into two forms:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Overt Behavior:</strong> <span class="highlight-orange">Observable</span> actions like talking, smiling, moving, or facial expressions.</li>
                <li><strong>Covert Behavior:</strong> <span class="highlight-blue">Unobservable</span> internal activities like thinking, feeling, and remembering (mental processes).</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="example-box">
                💡 <strong>Example:</strong> Ever cried during a sad movie? Your tears are <span class="highlight-orange">overt behavior</span>, while the empathy and sadness you feel inside are <span class="highlight-blue">covert mental processes</span>.
            </div>

            <h3>❓ What Makes Psychology Unique?</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Focus on Individuals:</strong> Unlike Sociology or Anthropology (which study group behavior and culture), psychology focuses primarily on the <span class="highlight-purple">individual</span>.</li>
                <li><strong>All Dimensions:</strong> Psychology covers <span class="highlight-purple">all aspects</span> of human behavior, rather than limiting itself to just one dimension.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 1.2 -->
    <section>
        <div class="card">
            <h2>🎯 1.2 The Four Goals of Psychology</h2>
            <p>Psychology seeks to fulfill four systematic goals when examining behavior:</p>

            <ol>
                <li><span class="highlight-purple">Description:</span> Observing and detailing behavior accurately (answers <em>"What is happening?"</em>).</li>
                <li><span class="highlight-green">Explanation:</span> Identifying causes and understanding reasons behind behavior (answers <em>"Why is it happening?"</em>).</li>
                <li><span class="highlight-blue">Prediction:</span> Determining how conditions will influence future behavior (answers <em>"When will it happen again?"</em>).</li>
                <li><span class="highlight-orange">Control:</span> Modifying or changing undesirable behavior into positive, desirable actions.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 1.3 -->
    <section>
        <div class="card">
            <h2>🏛️ 1.3 Historical Background</h2>
            <p>Psychology transitioned from philosophy to an independent science in <span class="highlight-purple">1879</span> when <span class="highlight-purple">Wilhelm Wundt</span> ("Father of Modern Psychology") established the first psychology laboratory at the University of Leipzig, Germany.</p>
            <p>Wundt introduced <span class="highlight-green">objective introspection</span> to scientifically record conscious mental experiences.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>📜 Early Schools of Psychology</h2>

            <h3>1. Structuralism</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Founder:</strong> Edward Titchener (based on Wundt's work).</li>
                <li><strong>Focus:</strong> Structure of the mind and basic elements of mental experiences (<span class="highlight-blue">sensations, images, feelings</span>).</li>
                <li><strong>Method:</strong> Introspection (looking inward at one's conscious experience).</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>2. Functionalism</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Founder:</strong> William James.</li>
                <li><strong>Focus:</strong> How the mind functions to help people adapt to real-world environments (work, play, survival).</li>
                <li><strong>Method:</strong> Questionnaires, mental tests, and objective observations.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>3. Gestalt Psychology</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Founders:</strong> Max Wertheimer and colleagues.</li>
                <li><strong>Focus:</strong> Studying the <span class="highlight-purple">whole mind</span> rather than isolated parts. ("The whole is greater than the sum of its parts").</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>4. Behaviorism</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Founder:</strong> John B. Watson (key contributors: B.F. Skinner, E. Thorndike).</li>
                <li><strong>Focus:</strong> Exclusively on <span class="highlight-orange">observable, measurable behavior</span>—rejecting hidden mental states.</li>
                <li><strong>Method:</strong> Direct observation and laboratory experiments.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>5. Psychoanalysis</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Founder:</strong> Sigmund Freud.</li>
                <li><strong>Focus:</strong> The <span class="highlight-blue">unconscious mind</span>, childhood conflicts, and hidden drives. Freud noted physical symptoms in hysteria patients often stemmed from unconscious emotional trauma.</li>
                <li><strong>Method:</strong> Clinical case studies, hypnosis, free association, and dream analysis.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>🧩 Modern Psychological Perspectives</h2>

            <div class="case-box">
                📋 <strong>Reference Case Study:</strong> On the playground, 6-year-old Sam pushes Sara off her bicycle and rides away on it.
            </div>

