    <section>
        <div class="card">
            <h2>💼 1.4 Subfields & Branches of Psychology</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Developmental Psychology:</strong> Lifespan physical, cognitive, and social changes.</li>
                <li><strong>Personality Psychology:</strong> Individual differences, enduring personal traits.</li>
                <li><strong>Social Psychology:</strong> Group dynamics, attitudes, and social interactions.</li>
                <li><strong>Cross-cultural Psychology:</strong> Cultural variations in behavior and thought.</li>
                <li><strong>Industrial/Organizational Psychology:</strong> Applying psychological rules to modern workplaces.</li>
                <li><strong>Forensic Psychology:</strong> Applying psychological principles within legal and criminal justice systems.</li>
                <li><strong>Educational Psychology:</strong> Enhancing teaching methods and learning environments.</li>
                <li><strong>Health Psychology:</strong> Psychological aspects of physical health and medical illness prevention.</li>
                <li><strong>Clinical Psychology:</strong> Diagnosing and treating severe psychological disorders.</li>
                <li><strong>Counseling Psychology:</strong> Assisting individuals with milder life problems and emotional adjustments.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 1.5 -->
    <section>
        <div class="card">
            <h2>🔬 1.5 Research Methods in Psychology</h2>

            <h3>Key Definitions</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Research:</strong> Systematic acquisition of knowledge to solve problems.</li>
                <li><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> A testable statement predicting relationships between variables.</li>
                <li><strong>Theory:</strong> Integrated principles explaining observed facts.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Scientific Method Steps</h3>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Define the Problem:</strong> Identify an observed occurrence needing explanation (e.g., student dropout rates).</li>
                <li><strong>Formulate Hypothesis:</strong> Propose a testable explanation.</li>
                <li><strong>Test Hypothesis:</strong> Collect data using suitable empirical methods.</li>
                <li><strong>Draw Conclusions:</strong> Analyze collected data to form general principles.</li>
                <li><strong>Report Results:</strong> Share methodologies and findings publicly for peer review.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Major Types of Research Methods</h3>

            <p><strong>A. Descriptive Research:</strong> Records observations directly without altering conditions.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Naturalistic Observation:</em> Subjects observed in natural habitats (limitations: observer bias, observer effect).</li>
                <li><em>Case Study:</em> In-depth examination of a single subject (limited generalizability).</li>
                <li><em>Survey:</em> Broad data gathering across large populations using standardized questions.</li>
            </ul>

            <p><strong>B. Correlational Research:</strong> Measures relationships between variables (Positive, Negative, or Zero).</p>
            <div class="example-box">
                ⚠️ <strong>Note:</strong> <span class="highlight-orange">Correlation does not equal causation.</span> Proving a link between smoking and lung cancer does not directly demonstrate causation without controlled experimentation.
            </div>

            <p><strong>C. Experimental Research:</strong> Demonstrates cause-and-effect relationships.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Independent Variable (IV):</strong> The manipulated factor.</li>
                <li><strong>Dependent Variable (DV):</strong> The measured outcome.</li>
                <li><strong>Experimental Group:</strong> Receives actual treatment.</li>
                <li><strong>Control Group:</strong> Baseline group used for comparison.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

